The trends of \(\Delta_{1-2}\), \(\Delta_{1-2}\), and \(\Delta_{1-2}\) are shown in Figure 3.9, for the same data set. The blue squares which depict \(\Delta_{1-2}\), show the trend indicated in equation 3.41. The first point is at pass number equals 0 and time difference equals -1.2, which indicates the constant U=-1.2 ns, and the whole set of points oscillate between -2.2 ns and 2.2 ns, which indicates the constant V=2.2 ns. The red up triangles which depict \(\Delta_{2-3}\) demonstrate the trend of equation 3.42, and the purple down triangles which depict \(\Delta_{3-1}\) demonstrate the trend shown in equation 3.43.

Although the offsets are not necessarily contained in the fit functions in the "1-1" approach, they are very well defined and can be simply accounted for during the subsequent TOF calibrations. For example, as shown above, after the fifth pass,\(\langle E_{1}^{(5)}\rangle=\langle t_{3}\rangle\), \(\langle E_{2}^{(5)}\rangle=\langle t_{2}\rangle\), \(\langle E_{3}^{(5)}\rangle=\langle t_{2}\rangle\), and \(\langle\Delta^{(5)}\rangle=\langle t_{2}\rangle-\langle t_{3}\rangle\)=\(V\). The offset \(V\) can be added to \(E_{1}^{(5)}\), then this effect is eliminated for all subsequent TOF analysis.

